  "epistemic_conclusion": "The empirical data support the conclusion that reconstructed CT image intensity alone does not provide sufficient contrast to recover the hypothesized Zone 2/Zone 3 boundary in the sampled frontoparietal dome regions. Among bone-classified voxels, the sampled dorsal compact cortex (Zone 3, mean=37349.8) and deep dome core (Zone 2, mean=37907.4) exhibit near-zero contrast (CNR = 0.0616 << 1.0, Bhattacharyya distance D_B = 0.0134, descriptive ROC AUC = 0.5132 across spatially correlated voxels), and this poor separability persists across a pre-specified threshold sensitivity range (T in [15000, 25000], CNR <= 0.26, descriptive AUC in [0.45, 0.55]). Analysis of unfiltered voxels reveals a non-trivial low-intensity fraction in the dome core (16.2% at Otsu threshold, with 11.8% < 10,000), representing internal lower-density voids, vascular canals, or matrix channels, consistent with published observations of spatial trabecular heterogeneity (Snively & Theodor 2011). The causal mechanism for the observed similarity among bone voxels remains uncertain; diagenetic mineral infill is a plausible explanation, but the analysis does not rule out reconstruction effects, residual beam hardening (11.34% cupping measured across the cross-section), partial volume averaging, or genuine tissue similarity in the sampled regions. Consequently, downstream Model B multi-zone material architecture cannot be directly segmented by thresholding or edge-detection operators from this CT dataset, and must instead be constructed through literature-informed geometric rules from published histological thin sections (Schott et al. 2011, Snively & Theodor 2011) mapped onto the verified canonical coordinate frame.",
